        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">South Indian megalithic culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, with key sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, with related graffiti marks in Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>; key sites Brahmagiri (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitradurga">Chitradurga</a> district, Karnataka), Adichanallur (Thoothukudi district, Tamil Nadu), Hallur, Sivagalai, Kodumanal</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>; secure Early Iron Age sites from c. 1000 BCE (Hallur, Adichanallur), with disputed earlier dates; graffiti marks c. 1000 BCE–300 CE; at Brahmagiri Wheeler dated the megalithic period to the 2nd century BCE–1st century CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">burials in stone cists, cist-circles, pit-circles and clay urns; iron tools and weapons; black and red ware pottery; graffiti symbols on pots; menhirs, dolmens and giant stone figures in human form</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated cemeteries and settlements (Adichanallur from 1876 and 2004–2006, Brahmagiri from 1947); radiocarbon and OSL dates; pots, tools and inscribed sherds from the graves</span></div>
        </div>
