    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the South Indian megalithic culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rice.jpg" alt="Rice" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Rice <span class="kid-tale-cr">Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 3,000 years ago, people in South India buried their dead in graves built or marked with big stones.</p><p>At one site, rice found in a burial urn with iron tools is more than 3,100 years old.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charcoal.jpg" alt="Charcoal" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Charcoal <span class="kid-tale-cr">Dati Bendo, European Commission, CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They used iron to make sickles for farming and spears and swords for fighting.</p><p>An iron furnace at Kodumanal, the oldest well-dated one in Tamil Nadu, is more than 2,400 years old.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>The dead were laid in stone boxes or in pits ringed by boulders, with pots, iron tools and beads.</p><p>At Adichanallur, people were buried in big clay pots called urns.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi.jpg" alt="Tamil-Brahmi" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Tamil-Brahmi <span class="kid-tale-cr">Symphonicallyu, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Many pots were scratched with symbols, and nobody knows for sure what they mean.</p><p>Later, writing called Tamil-Brahmi took the place of the old marks.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>On Brahmagiri, a granite hill in Chitradurga, archaeologists, people who dig up the past, found a burial ground of 300 tombs.</p><p>Today a museum is planned at Adichanallur, and a museum at Keezhadi is to show burial urns.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>’s megalithic culture is an archaeologists’ label for an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> way of burying the dead in graves built or marked with large stones, or in clay urns.</li><li>Its graves and settlements yield iron sickles, spears and swords, black and red ware pots, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and ornaments, and pots scratched with symbols whose meaning is still unknown.</li><li>Its dating is argued over, from securely dated sites of about 1000 BCE to disputed claims of far earlier iron; the graffiti marks disappear from graves around 300 CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When Adichanallur was first dug in 1876, a German ethnologist, Dr Jagor, took its pots, iron weapons and skulls to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, to the museum now called the Ethnological Museum of Berlin.</p></aside>
    </section>

